## Part 1 — Prepare the corpus

**1.1 Directory layout.** One directory per lab.

```
corpus/
  openai/
    docs/          OAI-PF-2023.pdf, OAI-PF-2025.pdf
    text/          OAI-PF-2023.txt, OAI-PF-2025.txt
    units/         OAI-PF-2023.units.jsonl
    crosswalk/     OAI-PF-2023_2025.crosswalk.jsonl
    coded/         OAI-PF-2025.content.claude.r1.jsonl
                   OAI-PF-2023_2025.change.gpt.r2.jsonl
  anthropic/
  deepmind/
decisions.md
codebook_v8.docx
```

Filename grammar: `{LAB}-{FRAMEWORK}-{YEAR}` for versions,
`{LAB}-{FRAMEWORK}-{YEAR_A}_{YEAR_B}` for transitions, then
`.{content|change}.{model}.r{n}.jsonl`. Everything downstream keys on these, so fix
them now and don't rename.

**1.2 Extract text and check it.** Frontier framework PDFs are table-heavy and the
tables carry a large share of the codeable content — Table 1 of the OpenAI v2
document holds most of its threshold language.

```bash
pdftotext -layout OAI-PF-2025.pdf OAI-PF-2025.txt
```

Then read the output where the tables are. `-layout` usually preserves cell
boundaries; without it, columns interleave and a table cell becomes an unusable
word salad. If a table comes out scrambled, transcribe those cells by hand into the
text file, marked with their row and column labels. This is tedious and worth it:
a scrambled table doesn't fail loudly, it just produces plausible-looking units
whose excerpts are nonsense.

**1.3 Strip what's out of scope.** Cover pages, tables of contents, running headers,
page numbers. The A1 prompt excludes these, but removing them first means fewer
tokens and fewer chances to slip through.

