## Part 4 — Scale

For N versions of a lab, chained adjacent (v1→v2→v3→v4):

| Run | Count | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A1 Segment | N | once per version, ever |
| A2 Align | N−1 | adjacent pairs only |
| B-content | N × models × repeats | per version |
| B-change | (N−1) × models × repeats | per transition |

**Adjacent pairs, not baseline-anchored.** Comparing everything against v1 destroys
non-monotonic change: a category dropped in v2 and reintroduced in v3 shows as "no
change" in a v1→v3 comparison. Your C03 and C05 vocabularies contain `reintroduced`
because the codebook expects exactly this, and DeepMind's Harmful Manipulation is a
real instance. The direction vocabularies presuppose adjacency — `tightened` is only
well-defined between consecutive states.

**Add one endpoint run per lab** (v_first → v_last), kept as a separate dataset and
never pooled with the chain. "Net position moved X over three years" and "there were
four transitions, three of which loosened" are different claims. The endpoint run
supports the first; the chain supports the second.

Human-code a subsample from **each lab**, not just the pilot. Alignment difficulty
varies with how much a document was restructured, and a lab that rewrote from
scratch will be harder in ways the pilot never showed you.

